    title_en: "Despite Shanghai Factory Becoming an Export Asset, Tesla Faces China Market Slowdown and US Geopolitical Risks",
    summary_en: "This article analyzes Tesla's operational status in China. Although the Shanghai factory produced 93,579 vehicles in June, marking a significant year-over-year increase of 38%, demonstrating its immense value as an export base. However, this high production capacity has not translated into stable domestic Chinese market sales. Chinese customers are showing fatigue with models like the Model 3, leading to sales that have declined for over a year in consecutive quarters. In the second quarter, over half of the vehicles (128,394 units) were exported to Europe, Canada, and other Asian markets, significantly exceeding the number sold within mainland China (126,157 units). Although Tesla benefits from China's low costs and government tax incentives, it faces several challenges, including policy changes in the US market. New US regulations will prohibit vehicle-to-internet software related to China starting in 2027, and related hardware starting in 2030. To mitigate geopolitical risks, Tesla has begun adjusting its supply chain, reducing its reliance on manufacturing vehicles in China for sale in the US.",
    tags_en: ["Tesla", "China Market", "Export Strategy", "Geopolitics", "Supply Chain"],
